ocabulary of the language,\" both of which correspond to the English term \"lexis.\" I'm now evaluating how well these existing lemmas capture the concept, starting with ذخيرة لغوية and its etymological foundations.\n\nThe term ذخيرة in linguistic contexts actually refers to a speaker's repertoire—the collection of linguistic items they have available—rather than the complete lexicon of a language. While it's a reasonable metaphorical fit for the stored linguistic wealth, the definition in المعجم الكبير suggests a somewhat different meaning, so this lemma needs diacritization updates. For مفردات اللغة, the word مفردات correctly denotes vocabulary items as the plural of مُفْرَدَة, though it typically implies a subset or inventory rather than the totality of a language's words, which ArabTerm confirms.\n\nThe inclusion of جميع (all) becomes crucial here to capture the hypernym relationship properly. I'm also checking several other candidates—الثَّرْوَةُ اللُّغَوِيَّة, مَخْزُونُ الأَلْفَاظ, المَادَّةُ اللُّغَوِيَّة, and اللَّفِيظَة—but none of these appear in the database as established terms for lexis, though some are used descriptively in pedagogical or grammatical contexts.\n\nThe real breakthrough is finding that مُعْجَم has strong evidence for \"مجموع مفردات اللغة\" in entry 727734 of المورد الحديث, which directly matches our synset definition, though it's broader since it also means dictionary. The phrase \"مجموع مفردات اللغة\" itself is the key match we're looking for, and now I need to determine which lemmas should actually represent this synset. I'm confirming that ذخيرة لغوية works with the caveat that it leans toward linguistic repertory rather than a strict lexicon, while مفردات اللغة is the strongest match. For the blind candidates I tested—الثروة اللغوية, مخزون الألفاظ, المادة اللغوية, and اللفيظة—none have supporting material and they either fail the multi-word expression test or carry different semantic weight.\n\nI'm considering whether to add معجم or ملفظة as lemmas, but معجم is already overloaded with the \"dictionary\" sense, and ملفظة refers to vocabulary as a performance list rather than the stored repertory we need. The two existing lemmas seem adequate, though I should verify this against the unified linguistic terminology reference.\n\nLooking at the المعجم الموحد لمصطلحات اللسانيات, ملفظة is explicitly defined as performance vocabulary—the actual words used in speech—distinct from the underlying lexical capacity. This distinction matters because the hypernym I'm working with (awn4-00023451-n) carries cognitive/knowledge semantics, but \"lexis\" as the complete word inventory of a language doesn't quite fit that frame.\n\nNow I'm moving into the candidate generation phase, reviewing blind proposals for Arabic lemmas that capture this concept without reference to existing WordNet entries. I've identified several candidates using different morphological patterns—compound nouns, construct phrases, and figurative language—each approaching the notion of linguistic wealth or word stock from slightly different angles.\n\nAfter examining the full synset definition, I'm noticing some structural issues that need flagging: the existing lemmas lack complete diacritical marks, \"ذخيرة لغوية\" seems to lean more toward the sense of a linguistic repertoire rather than the broader concept of all words in a language, and the parent category of perception/knowledge doesn't quite fit since lexis is fundamentally a linguistic concept, not an epistemological one. There's also the question of whether Standard Arabic makes the same distinctions that English does in this domain.\n\nNow I'm checking the evidence across different candidate terms. The dictionary sources confirm \"ذخيرة لغوية\" as a valid option with support from major references, though it carries the connotation of \"repertory\" rather than capturing the full scope of lexis. I'm also finding confirmation for \"مفردات اللغة\" through multiple linguistic databases, but several other candidates like \"الثروة اللغوية\" and \"مخزون الألفاظ\" aren't turning up material support. The term \"اللفيظة\" itself has no documented evidence.\n\nFor the confirmed lemmas, I need to add proper diacritization marks and note their grammatical structures—\"ذخيرة لغوية\" functions as a descriptive compound emphasizing the accumulation aspect within linguistic systems, while \"مفردات اللغة\" as an idiomatic construct focuses on individual lexical units and has broader general usage.\n\nNow I'm moving to Stage 4 content assessment. The current definition reads too much like a direct English translation, so I'm reformulating it in proper Arabic lexicographic style. The new terminological definition frames vocabulary as the sum of all linguistic units in a language, encompassing both semantically meaningful words and grammatical function words. I'm also establishing a linguistic definition that captures the phonetic inventory of the language.\n\nFor the examples, I'm illustrating how Arabic vocabulary represents a rich linguistic heritage spanning millennia, and how linguists work to document the lexical resources of endangered languages before they disappear. Now I'm moving into Stage 5 enrichment, beginning with the multi-word expression \"ذخيرة لغوية\" and its etymological roots.\n\nI'm examining the root of ذخر, which Ibn Faris describes as relating to the preservation and safeguarding of something. For لغوية, I'm tracing back to the root ل-غ-و, which is the classical root form that would appear in the Maqayis lexicon. I'm still missing the Maqayis entry for لغو, which appears for both لغوية and اللغة. Let me check my query budget before making another request—I've already done five queries for the headword lookup, English bridge, Arabic search, combined enrichment, and partial Maqayis data, so I have room for one more targeted lookup to complete this. I have enough budget left to run one more query for the لغو entry from Ibn Faris's Maqayis al-L